{"summary":"HB1234 would extend Tennessee’s school-library policies to classroom libraries. It defines a classroom library as materials a teacher obtains by donation, purchase, or other means and makes available to students assigned to the teacher’s classes. A school library collection would exclude course-curriculum materials and classroom-library materials. “Materials” would include books and other items and content in various formats, including digital applications and subscriptions.

Each local board of education and public charter school governing body would have to adopt a policy for developing and reviewing both school library collections and classroom libraries. The policy would have to provide a way for teachers, and for parents or legal guardians of students with access to a classroom library, to review and refer its materials for evaluation. It would also have to give those parents and guardians multiple chances during the school year to view books in their student’s classroom library.

The policy would have to prohibit teachers from knowingly or intentionally using a classroom library to get around the school’s library-collection policy. The bill gives as examples materials found by the state textbook and instructional materials quality commission to be inappropriate for the age and maturity of students who may access them, or materials found by the local board or charter-school governing body to be unsuitable for or inconsistent with the school’s educational mission. The bill also adds classroom libraries to specified references in other subsections of the cited law, without setting out those subsections’ full contents. It would take effect upon becoming law."}
